"At sunset, sunlight arrives almost sideways. Instead of crossing the air straight down, it travels through up to about 38 times as much air. Along that long path, almost all the blue is scattered away, and mostly orange and red light reaches you. Dust and smoke can make sunsets even redder. This reddened light even reaches the Moon during a total lunar eclipse and turns it a coppery red, as you learned in the astronomy series.": "4a31c34970d1",
