"Look up on a clear day in Changhua, and the sky is bright blue. Wait until evening, stand on the coast facing the Taiwan Strait, and the setting Sun glows orange and red. But sunlight looks white, and the air looks clear. So where does the blue come from, and why does the Sun change color at the end of the day?": "caacac059f9b",
"Look up on a clear day in Changhua, and the sky is bright blue. Wait until evening, stand on the coast facing the Taiwan Strait, and the setting Sun glows orange and red. But sunlight looks white, and the air looks clear. So where does the blue come from, and why does the Sun change color at the end of the day? In 1672, Isaac Newton reported that a glass prism splits white sunlight into a rainbow of colors. White light is really every color mixed together. Each color is a light wave with its own length: red waves are the longest we can see, and blue and violet waves are the shortest. In 1859, John Tyndall showed that tiny particles scatter blue light more than red, and in 1871, Lord Rayleigh worked out the exact rule. Here is Rayleigh's rule. Air molecules are far smaller than light waves. When sunlight hits them, they scatter a little of it in every direction, and the shorter the wave, the more it is scattered. Blue light is scattered several times more than red light. As sunlight crosses the air, blue light bounces off in all directions, so wherever you look in the sky, blue light is coming toward your eyes. Violet waves are even shorter, so why isn't the sky violet? Sunlight has less violet than blue to begin with, some violet is absorbed high in the air, and our eyes are less sensitive to violet. The mix that reaches us looks sky blue. Clouds are different. Their water droplets are much bigger than light waves, so they scatter all colors about equally, and clouds look white or gray. At sunset, sunlight arrives almost sideways. Instead of crossing the air straight down, it travels through up to about 38 times as much air. Along that long path, almost all the blue is scattered away, and mostly orange and red light reaches you. Dust and smoke can make sunsets even redder. This reddened light even reaches the Moon during a total lunar eclipse and turns it a coppery red, as you learned in the astronomy series. Without air, there would be no blue sky. On the Moon, which has almost no air, the sky is black even in the daytime. On Mars, the thin air is full of fine dust, so the daytime sky looks orange or reddish, and the sky around the setting Sun turns blue-gray, the opposite of Earth. Next time you see a blue sky or a red sunset, remember that you are seeing sunlight bounce off the air above you.": "e40c4e539fbb",
